<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · song</p><h1 class="enc-title">We Wish You a Merry Christmas</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">date unknown; a closely related verse from the 1830s; the famous version published <a href="/years/1935">1935</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">&quot;We Wish You a Merry <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christmas">Christmas</a>&quot; is an English <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christmas-carol">Christmas carol</a>, and its famous version comes from the West Country of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>. The greeting &quot;A merry Christmas and a happy New Year&quot; is recorded from the early 18th century, but the history of the carol itself has yet to be made clear.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The carol is listed as numbers 230 and 9681 in the Roud Folk Song Index. Its origin probably lies in the English custom in which wealthy people gave <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christmas">Christmas</a> treats to carollers on Christmas Eve, such as figgy pudding, which in the West Country meant a raisin or plum pudding, not necessarily one containing figs.</p><p>The song is absent from the collections of Davies Gilbert and William Sandys, from the anthologies of Sylvester and Husk, and from The Oxford Book of Carols of 1928. The New Oxford Book of Carols of 1992 calls it English traditional and describes it as the remnant of an envoie much used by wassailers and other luck visitors, giving no source or date.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>A closely related verse wishes a merry <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christmas">Christmas</a> and a happy new year, with a pocket full of money and a cellar full of beer. It was sung by mummers, townsfolk who went from door to door singing to request gifts, and Charlotte Yonge gives an example in her story The Christmas Mummers of 1858. Versions survived into the twentieth century and were recorded by collectors, among them George Dunn and Mary Evans of Quarry Bank, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/staffordshire">Staffordshire</a>; all use completely different tunes from the West Country carol.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>The composer, conductor and organist Arthur Warrell, a lecturer at the University of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bristol">Bristol</a> from 1909, is responsible for the carol's popularity. He arranged the tune in four parts for the University of Bristol Madrigal Singers, who performed it on 6 December 1935, and Oxford University Press published it that year. His words begin with I rather than we. It was republished in Carols for Choirs in 1961 and remains widely performed. The best-known version was completely unknown outside the West Country before Warrell.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “We Wish You a Merry Christmas” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>&quot;<b>We Wish You a Merry Christmas</b>&quot; is an English Christmas carol, and its famous version comes from the West Country of England.</blockquote><a href="/songs/we-wish-you-a-merry-christmas">We Wish You a Merry Christmas</a></li></ul></section>
